readings.c1s1:
¶
'''blood''' (blud), ''v. i.'' [< ''blood'', ''n.''] '''1†.''' To let blood from; bleed by opening a vein. ''Johnson.''—'''2†.''' To stain with blood.
{{smaller block|<poem>Reach out their spears afar,
And ''blood'' their points to prove their partnership in war.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Fables.}}}}
Hence—'''3.''' To give a taste of blood; inure to the sight of blood.
{{smaller block|It was most important too that his troops should be ''blooded.'' {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., ix.}}
He [the deerhound] must be made steady from all “riot,” and, if possible, should be taken up in couples to the death of a deer once or twice and ''blooded'', so as to make him understand the nature of the scent.
{{right|''Dogs of Great Britain and America'', p. 221.}}}}
'''4†.''' To heat the blood of; excite; exasperate.
{{smaller block|The auxiliary forces of French and English were much ''blooded'' one against another. {{right|''Bacon'', Hist. Hen. VII.}}}}
'''5†.''' To victimize; extract money from (a person); bleed. [Slang.]

'''blood-baptism''' (blud′bap″tizm), ''n.'' A term applied by the early Christians to the martyrdom of those converts who had not been baptized. See ''baptism of blood'', under ''baptism.''

'''blood-bespotted''' (blud′bẹ̄-spot″ed), ''a.'' Spotted with blood.
{{smaller block|O ''blood-bespotted'' Neapolitan. {{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., v. 1.}}}}

'''blood-boltered†''' (blud′bōl″tėrd), ''a.'' [< ''blood'' + ''boltered'', pp. of ''bolter'', a rare word: see ''bolter''<sup>4</sup>.] Clotted or clogged with blood.
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''blood-bolter’d'' Banquo smiles upon me.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Macbeth, iv. 1.}}
In Warwickshire, when a horse, sheep, or other animal}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''blood-mare''' (blud′mãr), ''n.'' A mare of blooded breed; a female blood-horse.

'''blood-money''' (blud′mun″i), ''n.'' Money paid as the price of blood. {{smaller|(''a'') Compensation or reward for bringing about the death of another, either by bringing a capital charge against him or by giving such testimony as will lead to conviction. (''b'') Compensation formerly, and still in some non-Christian countries, paid to the next of kin for the killing of a relative.}}

'''blood-pheasant''' (blud′fez″ant), ''n.'' A bird of the genus ''Ithaginis'' (which see).

'''blood-plaque''' (blud′plak), ''n.'' Same as ''blood-plate.''

'''blood-plate''' (blud′plāt), ''n.'' One of the minute discoidal bodies found in large numbers in the blood of mammals. {{smaller|They are from one fourth to one half the size of the red corpuscles, and are many times more numerous than the white corpuscles. See ''blood'' and ''blood-corpuscle.'' Also called ''hematoblasts of Hayem'', and ''corpuscles'' or ''elementary particles of Zimmermann.''}}

'''blood-poisoning''' (blud′poi″zn-ing), ''n.'' See ''toxemia.''

'''blood-pudding''' (blud′pu̇d″ing), ''n.'' Same as ''black-pudding.''

'''blood-red''' (blud′red), ''a.'' [< ME. ''blodrede'', < AS. ''blōdreád'' (= D. ''bloedrood'' = G. ''blutroth'' = Icel. ''blōdhraudhr'' = Sw. Dan. ''blodröd''), < ''blōd'', blood, + ''reád'', red.] Blood-colored; red with blood.
{{smaller block|<poem>He wrapped his colours round his breast,
On a ''blood-red'' field of Spain.</poem>{{right|''Hemans.''}}}}

'''Blood-red hand,''' {{smaller|in ''her.'', the ''badge of Ulster.'' See ''badge''<sup>1</sup> and ''baronet.''}}
{{smaller block|The event which was to place the ''blood-red hand'' of the Newcome baronetcy on his own brougham.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes.}}}}

'''Blood-red heat,''' {{smaller|the degree of heat, shown by the color,}}

