¶
'''blood-relation''' (blud′rẹ̄-lā″shọn), ''n.'' One related by blood or descent; a kinsman.

'''blood-relationship''' (blud′rẹ̄-lā″shọn-ship), ''n.'' Consanguinity; kinship.

{{smaller block|The hypothesis of differing gradations of ''blood-relationship.''
{{right|''Claus'', Zoölogy (trans.), p. 157.}}}}

'''bloodroot''' (blud′röt), ''n.'' '''1.''' The tormentil (''Potentilla Tormentilla'') of Europe and northern Asia: named from the color of its root, which
[[FIGURE]]
{{c|{{smaller|Bloodroot (''Sanguinaria Canadensis'').}}}}
is rich in a red coloring matter. It is also rich in tannin, and has been used as an astringent.—'''2.''' The common name in the United States of a papaveraceous herb, ''Sanguinaria Canadensis'', one of the earliest spring flowers. {{smaller|Its fleshy roots yield a dark-red juice, are bitter and acrid, and contain a peculiar alkaloid, sanguinarin. It is used in medicine as a stimulant, expectorant, and emetic.}}

'''blood-sacrifice''' (blud′sak″ri-fīs), ''n.'' A sacrifice made with shedding of blood; the sacrifice of a living being.

{{smaller block|<poem>Cannot my body, nor ''blood-sacrifice'',
Entreat you to your wonted furtherance?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 3.}}}}
